    "A bathroom or WC has no door, opens onto a kitchen, or is a passage.",
    "Three cases, each with its own message. (1) The wet room's only connector is a leaf-less `opening` onto a habitable room (living, dining, kitchen, bedroom, office), so it cannot be closed. (2) A connector joins it straight to a kitchen or dining room with no lobby between (GB 50096-2011 5.4.3, cited as advice, not compliance; ResPlan: about 0.3 % of bath doors open off a kitchen); a kitchen that is also the living room counts only where the door opens onto its kitchen, a sink, hob, oven, fridge, worktop, island or dishwasher on its floor nearer the doorway's face than `wetDoorKitchenProximityMm` (default 1500 mm, compared in whole mm; a microwave is portable and does not locate the kitchen), and one with no such fixture is declared to the lint coverage report (`no-kitchen-fixtures`) instead; a kitchen that is also the dining room counts like a kitchen alone; a living room or a living/dining room with no kitchen use does not count. (3) Two or more connectors join it to two different rooms outside a private suite (not a bedroom, bath, WC or storage room), so the way between them runs through it; when one of them is reached only through the wet room, `W_ROOM_VIA_PRIVATE` reports that instead. A room with no classified use is skipped. Cases (1), (3) and (2)'s dining room describe a home: on a plan that reads as non-residential (a café, restaurant or clinic label; or, with no bedroom or living room, a room whose use is `office` or a shop, lobby, reception, waiting or meeting label) they are not judged, and the lint coverage report says so (`non-dwelling`); a door onto a kitchen is judged on every plan, since a WC opening onto a room where food is handled is a fault in a café too. The `residential-basic` profile judges every plan as a dwelling.",
    "(1) Hang a leaf in it: write `door` in place of `opening`, at least 700 mm wide, or move its way in onto a hall. (2) Re-host the door onto a hall or corridor the wet room shares a door-wide edge with (the hints name each one, measured on centrelines), or put a lobby at least 900 mm clear between them; in an open-plan kitchen/living room, keep the kitchen's fixtures at least `wetDoorKitchenProximityMm` from the doorway. (3) Where one of the two rooms already has its own door to a room reached without the wet room, remove the wet room's door to it; where the two share a door-wide straight edge, re-host the wet room's second door onto it; otherwise run a corridor at least 900 mm clear between them. There is no machine fix: a door is a geometric decision (ADR 0005).",
    'plan "Bath off the kitchen" {\n  units mm\n  wall exterior thickness 200 { (0,0) (6000,0) (6000,3000) (0,3000) close }\n  wall partition thickness 100 { (4000,0) (4000,3000) }\n  room at (0,0) size 4000x3000 label "Kitchen"\n  room at (4000,0) size 2000x3000 label "Bathroom"\n  door at (2000,3000) width 900 wall exterior\n  door at (4000,1500) width 700 wall partition   # lint: the bath opens straight into the kitchen\n}',
